# answered by claude-opus-5-5, prompt /lib/phil/senses b350a3107fa4, 2026-10-19
god_exists	one
infinite_goodness	one
evil_exists	one
# infinite_goodness: someone could say "infinite" shifts between S1 and S2. In S1 it would mean unbounded in extent, so that a contrary has no room left. In S2 it would mean unlimited in perfection, with nothing lacking.
# The text does not draw that distinction, though. S1 states a general principle about contraries, and S2 applies it to the case of God's goodness. A general claim applied to a case is one sense, so I read it as one sense and found no bridge.
